1
00:00:00,680 --> 00:00:02,920
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,160 --> 00:00:04,160
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:07,080
My name is Catherine and it's the holiday season, Marco.

4
00:00:07,560 --> 00:00:15,360
People are eating large meals with their families and I think oftentimes when you cook a lot of food, you have something extra at the end.

5
00:00:15,460 --> 00:00:16,000
What do we call that?

6
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:16,520
That's right.

7
00:00:16,740 --> 00:00:18,680
Today we're going to talk about leftovers.

8
00:00:19,780 --> 00:00:24,120
That's the extra food after your meal that you eat later, maybe the next day.

9
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Exactly.

10
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:32,200
So, you know, during Christmas or during some holidays, we cook so much food that obviously we can't eat it all.

11
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:35,940
So we put it back in the refrigerator and then we eat it later.

12
00:00:36,120 --> 00:00:37,080
So those are called leftovers.

13
00:00:37,080 --> 00:00:38,080
All right.

14
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:42,980
So let's listen to this dialogue about leftovers and we'll be back in a moment to discuss what's happening in the dialogue.

15
00:00:49,720 --> 00:00:51,640
What's for dinner?

16
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:52,640
Leftovers.

17
00:00:52,640 --> 00:00:53,640
What?

18
00:00:53,640 --> 00:00:54,360
Leftovers of what and from when?

19
00:00:55,400 --> 00:00:55,840
From last night.

20
00:00:56,500 --> 00:01:02,520
I took the leftover turkey, mixed it with some diced peppers and onions, add a little bit of mayonnaise and made some sandwiches.

21
00:01:03,340 --> 00:01:04,520
Isn't that dangerous though?

22
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:09,200
I mean, bacteria and germs reproducing on food that was left out or reheated.

23
00:01:09,360 --> 00:01:14,920
Well, I didn't leave the turkey out at room temperature for more than an hour and I refrigerated soon after we finished eating.

24
00:01:15,720 --> 00:01:20,000
Also when reheating, I put it in the oven for 15 minutes at 100 degrees Celsius.

25
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:21,840
Well, okay.

26
00:01:22,300 --> 00:01:23,760
I'm just afraid of getting food poisoning.

27
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:25,360
Don't worry about it.

28
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:27,880
Making a new meal out of leftovers is almost an art.

29
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:32,480
Not only do you save money, but you also get to be creative and have something different to eat.

30
00:01:33,280 --> 00:01:34,280
All right.

31
00:01:37,560 --> 00:01:38,720
So a lot of vocab there.

32
00:01:38,900 --> 00:01:41,060
Let's take a look at it in Language Takeaway.

33
00:01:43,690 --> 00:01:44,870
Language Takeaway.

34
00:01:45,280 --> 00:01:46,280
All right.

35
00:01:46,950 --> 00:01:51,290
So Marco, there's one cooking term really in this dialogue that is, it's a little bit different from the others.

36
00:01:51,550 --> 00:01:52,370
So let's start with that.

37
00:01:52,910 --> 00:01:53,450
It's diced.

38
00:01:54,210 --> 00:01:54,630
That's right.

39
00:01:54,930 --> 00:01:58,430
We were talking about taking some peppers or diced peppers.

40
00:01:58,890 --> 00:02:06,810
So when you dice a vegetable or anything that you're cooking, basically you're making little pieces, little squares almost.

41
00:02:07,150 --> 00:02:07,650
That's right.

42
00:02:07,910 --> 00:02:09,870
So you can cut something many different ways.

43
00:02:10,690 --> 00:02:18,210
You can cut big pieces and small pieces, but diced peppers, diced is a specific way to cut something in small, like you say, small squares.

44
00:02:18,930 --> 00:02:24,890
And so in recipes, you will often see this instruction, dice the peppers, or in this case, the peppers are diced.

45
00:02:25,390 --> 00:02:25,870
That's right.

46
00:02:26,250 --> 00:02:26,470
All right.

47
00:02:26,730 --> 00:02:30,670
So we took some turkey, mix it with diced peppers and a lot of things.

48
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:34,580
And the roommate was a little bit concerned about bacteria and germs.

49
00:02:34,720 --> 00:02:35,720
All right.

50
00:02:35,720 --> 00:02:39,380
So bacteria and germs, these sound like very scientific words.

51
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:44,740
Bacteria is something that grows on, well, everything really.

52
00:02:45,120 --> 00:02:51,320
But if you leave food out, for example, it gets bacteria or germs are things that can make you sick.

53
00:02:51,700 --> 00:02:52,080
That's right.

54
00:02:52,460 --> 00:02:56,560
So we need to avoid these things in the kitchen because they make us unhealthy.

55
00:02:57,680 --> 00:03:00,000
And that's why we wash our hands and things like that.

56
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:02,800
But when you have food that's left out, the risk is what, Marco?

57
00:03:03,300 --> 00:03:05,560
Is that you get bacteria or germs on it.

58
00:03:05,880 --> 00:03:06,880
Yeah.

59
00:03:06,880 --> 00:03:08,520
So it's dangerous, especially with meat.

60
00:03:08,680 --> 00:03:13,960
If you leave it out, it's very, very dangerous because you can get some bacteria that will make you sick.

61
00:03:14,680 --> 00:03:23,220
And the reason why we're a little bit concerned, especially with meats, is if you leave it out after cooking it at room temperature.

62
00:03:23,920 --> 00:03:24,220
All right.

63
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:26,940
So room temperature is the temperature of a normal room.

64
00:03:27,680 --> 00:03:33,060
Maybe it's 65 degrees or what's 25 degrees Celsius.

65
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:36,700
It's the temperature of the inside of a room.

66
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:40,400
And so it's not cold, it's not hot, it's just normal.

67
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:44,680
And when we leave food out, especially meat, like you're saying, we have to be careful.

68
00:03:44,680 --> 00:03:51,680
You can't leave it out for very long if it's something like eggs or milk or what we call perishables.

69
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:51,880
That's right.

70
00:03:52,680 --> 00:03:56,440
So actually, here comes the other two words that are a little bit of a contrast.

71
00:03:56,440 --> 00:04:00,320
We're going to talk about food that is reheated or refrigerated.

72
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:01,320
All right.

73
00:04:01,470 --> 00:04:07,730
So the roommate is explaining, don't worry, we have done these things to this food so it's safe.

74
00:04:08,250 --> 00:04:13,470
So one thing you can do to food to make it safe to kill germs is to reheat it.

75
00:04:13,830 --> 00:04:17,090
That means, well, you know the word heated, right?

76
00:04:17,310 --> 00:04:18,790
It's to make something hot.

77
00:04:19,090 --> 00:04:20,570
How is that different from reheated?

78
00:04:20,830 --> 00:04:23,830
Well, because you cooked it already, so it was warm.

79
00:04:24,270 --> 00:04:27,250
Now it went a little bit cold because it's at room temperature.

80
00:04:27,970 --> 00:04:36,090
So in order to make sure that it's safe to eat and you kill that bacteria, you reheat it so you kind of cook it again a little bit.

81
00:04:36,310 --> 00:04:37,070
Yeah, you cook it again.

82
00:04:37,350 --> 00:04:44,670
So with leftovers, we often have to reheat things because they were hot, then they were cold and now we want them hot again.

83
00:04:44,890 --> 00:04:45,210
That's right.

84
00:04:45,290 --> 00:04:45,890
So we reheat them.

85
00:04:46,190 --> 00:04:48,570
The opposite of that would be refrigerate.

86
00:04:48,850 --> 00:04:50,490
Right, and this is what you should do.

87
00:04:50,490 --> 00:04:58,940
If you take something and put it in the refrigerator, what you are doing, the verb, the action is to refrigerate it.

88
00:04:59,380 --> 00:04:59,940
That's right.

89
00:05:00,120 --> 00:05:02,600
So to refrigerate something means to make it cold.

90
00:05:03,680 --> 00:05:05,760
You know, there are important things that we refrigerate.

91
00:05:05,800 --> 00:05:10,200
We refrigerate eggs, we refrigerate milk, we refrigerate butter.

92
00:05:10,600 --> 00:05:13,760
These are things that can make us sick if we don't make them cold.

93
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:14,460
Exactly.

94
00:05:15,180 --> 00:05:16,780
So those are the two words.

95
00:05:17,100 --> 00:05:25,440
Now obviously, it's a little bit different because the prefix to reheat means to do it again while to refrigerate means you're just making it cold.

96
00:05:25,760 --> 00:05:26,760
Yeah.

97
00:05:26,760 --> 00:05:29,520
Well, they're important terms and again, we also have the adjectives.

98
00:05:29,520 --> 00:05:31,780
This is reheated or refrigerated.

99
00:05:32,660 --> 00:05:36,960
But there are another couple of phrases that are really important so let's take a listen to those in Fluency Builder.

100
00:05:39,280 --> 00:05:39,580
Fluency Builder.

101
00:05:40,830 --> 00:05:46,070
All right, so the first phrase we're going to take a look at is when one of the roommates says, hey, what's for dinner?

102
00:05:46,730 --> 00:05:47,450
What's for dinner?

103
00:05:47,730 --> 00:05:50,710
All right, this is a very, very standard phrase.

104
00:05:50,990 --> 00:05:53,250
This is something you'll hear at home, you'll hear with your friends.

105
00:05:54,270 --> 00:05:57,970
It's usually something we say that when someone is cooking food for dinner.

106
00:05:58,310 --> 00:05:58,690
That's right.

107
00:05:58,870 --> 00:06:01,510
We're asking what are we going to eat for dinner?

108
00:06:01,930 --> 00:06:03,230
Mom, what's for dinner?

109
00:06:03,760 --> 00:06:04,760
Exactly.

110
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:08,000
I used to say that every single day when I came home from school.

111
00:06:08,060 --> 00:06:10,380
Well, obviously, you don't want to say the long phrase.

112
00:06:10,960 --> 00:06:13,060
What are we going to have for dinner tonight?

113
00:06:13,240 --> 00:06:14,280
So you just say, what's for dinner?

114
00:06:14,500 --> 00:06:14,960
What's for dinner?

115
00:06:15,340 --> 00:06:19,280
And then the answer would be lasagna or turkey sandwiches.

116
00:06:19,860 --> 00:06:20,580
Right, exactly.

117
00:06:21,360 --> 00:06:34,000
Now when they started talking about the leftover turkey, he was a little bit concerned as we said about bacteria and germs because it usually happens that you get this on food that was left out.

118
00:06:34,630 --> 00:06:38,690
All right, so the verb here is to leave out, to leave something out.

119
00:06:39,350 --> 00:06:42,990
It means to leave something or put something at room temperature.

120
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:52,960
So Marco, you and I are eating turkey, it's Thanksgiving, and we start to have coffee and dessert and the turkey is still in the room, at room temperature.

121
00:06:52,960 --> 00:06:54,720
We did not refrigerate it.

122
00:06:54,720 --> 00:06:55,690
That means it was left out.

123
00:06:56,120 --> 00:06:57,210
How long was it left out?

124
00:06:57,440 --> 00:06:59,840
Well, maybe two hours.

125
00:06:59,840 --> 00:07:02,840
That means so it's not in a refrigerator.

126
00:07:02,840 --> 00:07:02,980
That's right.

127
00:07:03,840 --> 00:07:04,240
That's exactly what it means.

128
00:07:04,840 --> 00:07:06,900
So maybe you go to sleep and you left the turkey out.

129
00:07:07,200 --> 00:07:09,320
It's still on the table maybe at room temperature.

130
00:07:09,640 --> 00:07:12,620
Oh no, we can't eat it if we left it out for like 12 hours.

131
00:07:13,080 --> 00:07:14,080
Exactly.

132
00:07:14,080 --> 00:07:17,440
All right, so it's not good to leave food out.

133
00:07:17,440 --> 00:07:24,720
And now what happens is if you do eat food that was left out for too long, you could get food poisoning.

134
00:07:25,480 --> 00:07:30,020
Now this is very serious and it's something that you should be very careful about when you're cooking.

135
00:07:30,840 --> 00:07:34,330
Food poisoning is getting sick from your food.

136
00:07:34,560 --> 00:07:40,680
There are different kinds of food poisoning, but usually it makes you throw up, it makes your stomach hurt, it's quite painful.

137
00:07:40,680 --> 00:07:41,680
That's right.

138
00:07:41,680 --> 00:07:44,450
And this is why we say you should wash your hands before you cook.

139
00:07:44,880 --> 00:07:50,670
You should make sure that you refrigerate meat because you want to avoid food poisoning.

140
00:07:51,640 --> 00:07:52,640
That's right.

141
00:07:52,640 --> 00:07:57,390
And it comes from the noun poison, a poison, which is something that is harmful for your body.

142
00:07:58,120 --> 00:07:59,120
That's right.

143
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:06,600
There are many different kinds of poison, but food poisoning means becoming sick, your body getting sick after you eat something bad.

144
00:08:06,600 --> 00:08:06,850
That's right.

145
00:08:07,600 --> 00:08:08,600
All right.

146
00:08:08,600 --> 00:08:17,760
So with all this stuff here, we can review everything one last time.

147
00:08:17,760 --> 00:08:18,260
What's for dinner?

148
00:08:19,360 --> 00:08:19,660
Leftovers.

149
00:08:20,160 --> 00:08:21,160
What?

150
00:08:21,160 --> 00:08:22,480
Leftovers of what and from when?

151
00:08:23,440 --> 00:08:23,960
From last night.

152
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:30,540
I took the leftover turkey, mixed it with some diced peppers and onions, add a little bit of mayonnaise and made some sandwiches.

153
00:08:31,360 --> 00:08:32,540
Isn't that dangerous though?

154
00:08:32,640 --> 00:08:37,720
I mean, bacteria and germs reproducing on food that was left out or reheated.

155
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:42,940
Well I didn't leave the turkey at a room temperature for more than an hour and I refrigerated soon after we finished eating.

156
00:08:44,160 --> 00:08:48,040
Also when reheating, I put it in the oven for 15 minutes at 100 degrees Celsius.

157
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:49,880
Well, okay.

158
00:08:50,240 --> 00:08:51,780
I'm just afraid of getting food poisoning.

159
00:08:52,260 --> 00:08:53,380
Don't worry about it.

160
00:08:53,860 --> 00:08:55,920
Making a new meal out of leftovers is almost an art.

161
00:08:56,680 --> 00:09:00,520
Not only do you save money, but you also get to be creative and have something different to eat.

162
00:09:04,980 --> 00:09:09,980
All right, so this is actually good advice about leftovers, about having something to eat.

163
00:09:11,240 --> 00:09:15,920
But at the same time, I don't know how many people actually are very careful with these things.

164
00:09:16,880 --> 00:09:17,880
For example, pizza.

165
00:09:17,880 --> 00:09:20,360
Sometimes you leave pizza out on the table all night and the next morning you have some pizza.

166
00:09:21,200 --> 00:09:22,530
I always refrigerate my pizza.

167
00:09:23,130 --> 00:09:31,830
I know people who leave their pizza out and then eat it, but there's cheese and sausage and you want to refrigerate that stuff.

168
00:09:31,990 --> 00:09:35,330
So I always wrap it in plastic and put it on a plate in the fridge.

169
00:09:35,630 --> 00:09:35,990
Oh, okay.

170
00:09:36,110 --> 00:09:36,830
You wrap it in plastic.

171
00:09:37,390 --> 00:09:41,330
Well, because in the refrigerator there's also germs, so you need to make sure you cover your food.

172
00:09:41,920 --> 00:09:43,520
Right, right, right.

173
00:09:43,520 --> 00:09:49,210
What about, have you ever had a bad case of food poisoning because of having leftovers?

174
00:09:49,680 --> 00:09:49,970
No.

175
00:09:50,680 --> 00:09:53,100
I've had food poisoning, but not because of leftovers.

176
00:09:53,400 --> 00:09:55,980
Because as I said before, I wrap things, I put them in the refrigerator.

177
00:09:56,260 --> 00:10:00,600
I'm very careful because I don't want to get sick or I don't want to get other people sick.

178
00:10:01,160 --> 00:10:06,000
But Thanksgiving or Christmas, you have to give food to many other people.

179
00:10:06,040 --> 00:10:07,040
I don't want to make anyone sick.

180
00:10:07,640 --> 00:10:17,320
And actually, you can get food poisoning not only from leftovers, but from maybe food that maybe was expired.

181
00:10:17,320 --> 00:10:21,880
Sometimes cheese maybe can be expired, you didn't realize it, and you get food poisoning.

182
00:10:21,880 --> 00:10:25,640
Or from people who are cooking with things that are not clean.

183
00:10:25,640 --> 00:10:30,240
So you have something that's not clean or food that's not fresh and it makes you very sick.

184
00:10:30,240 --> 00:10:31,240
So it's not good for your health.

185
00:10:31,240 --> 00:10:32,240
So you have to be careful.

186
00:10:32,240 --> 00:10:33,240
That's right.

187
00:10:33,240 --> 00:10:39,480
So it's an interesting thing, especially with, like I said, so many holidays where you cook so much food that people have a lot of leftovers.

188
00:10:39,480 --> 00:10:46,720
And not only that, but even at restaurants, maybe you order too much, so you have leftovers you want to take home to your dog or to have later.

189
00:10:46,760 --> 00:10:47,560
I love leftovers.

190
00:10:47,720 --> 00:10:51,680
Let us know, do you have leftovers in your country?

191
00:10:51,680 --> 00:10:54,320
Are there things that you should and shouldn't do when cooking?

192
00:10:55,320 --> 00:10:55,560
Let us know.

193
00:10:56,320 --> 00:10:57,320
Our website is EnglishPod.com.

194
00:10:57,320 --> 00:10:58,320
All right, we'll see you guys there.

195
00:10:58,320 --> 00:11:00,320
Bye for now.
